{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And the woman saw that the tree was good for food</b> etc. Our Rabbis of blessed memory disagreed concerning this matter — what tree it was. Some say it was a fig tree, as it says ״ויתפרו עלה תאנה״ — in that with which they sinned they were repaired. Some say it was a vine, and this is what the Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: she squeezed grapes and brought them to him; and because its appearance was red like blood, therefore his blood was spilled, and the blood of all the world — measure for measure. And therefore her blood was spilled — she was given the blood of menstruation, which is an illness in the nature of a woman from time to time. And this is the secret of the verse (Deuteronomy 32:14): ״ודם ענב תשתה חמר״ — which called the drink that comes from grapes 'blood,' because it brought blood to the world. And some say it was an etrog. And so they said in the Midrash: What was that tree from which Adam and Eve ate? Rabbi Yehudah says: they were grapes. Rabbi Abba of Akko says: it was an etrog. Rabbi Azariah and Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi say: Heaven forbid — the Holy One, blessed be He, did not reveal that tree to any person, nor will He ever reveal it. See what is written (Leviticus 20:16): ״ואשה אשר תקרב אל כל בהמה לרבעה אותה והרגת את האשה ואת הבהמה״ — if a person sinned, what did the animal sin? Rather, so that the animal should not pass through the market and people would say, 'This is the animal on account of which so-and-so was stoned.' And if concerning its offspring the Holy One, blessed be He, was sparing of His honor, how much more so. Behold, this is the disagreement of the Sages in this matter." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: it was an etrog, and it is alluded to in the verse, as it says ״ונחמד העץ להשכיל״, and the translation of Onkelos is ״ומרגג״, for it is a desirable and beautiful fruit, and its nature is warm and rules over the intellect (שכל). And likewise the translation of ״הדר״ is אתרוגין. And from this He mentioned in His curse: ״וקוץ ודרדר תצמיח לך״." "<b>And that it is a desire to the eyes.</b> It is possible to explain that the good and the desire and the desirableness mentioned in the verse — which are attributes of the senses — are all drawn toward the word להשכיל. And because the human being was at first entirely intellectual (שכלי), the Scripture comes to clarify that all their intent and all their actions and desires through their senses were nothing but להשכיל — to gain understanding, in the manner of what is written (Psalms 38:10): ״ה' נגדך כל תאותי״. And the intellect compelled him to come to eat of what the Holy One, blessed be He, had withheld from him, and his intent was להשכיל. Nevertheless, he transgressed the will of his Creator and His commandment; for just as the intellect told him that the tree was desirable to gain understanding, he should have understood that it is not fitting to transgress the commandment of the Master of all. And the meaning is that the evil impulse (יצה\"ר) had not yet been created in him — for he was entirely intellectual without the evil impulse, except that the evil impulse seduced him and misled him by means of Eve. But after they ate of the fruit, a new desire was renewed in him and he became clothed in it — and that is the lust of intercourse, which his eating caused; and it is the impulse of transgression that entered him, which he did not have at first. And let it not be difficult for you to say: but those who stumble in sins and transgress the covenant do so on account of the evil impulse — and if so, what brought him to transgress, given that he was entirely intellectual without an evil impulse? For this quality is also found among the intellects (the angels): even though they have no evil impulse, they sometimes deviate from the proper path, as is known regarding the angels of Sodom." "<b>And likewise</b> in the Midrash, the Rabbis of blessed memory [expound]: ״כי לא ישא לפשעכם״ — because he is of the class that does not sin. And what is understood from this is that among some of the other angels there are those who sin, and so it is written (Job 4:18): ״ובמלאכיו ישים תהלה״." "<b>And she gave also to her husband with her.</b> The word עמה ('with her') teaches an evil thought in the woman: she wanted him to eat as well, because if perhaps some harm would befall her from this eating, he too would be harmed along with her." "<b>And in the Midrash.</b> She gave also to every beast, wild animal, and bird — and this is the force of the amplifying גם ('also'). Except for one bird whose name is חול (the hol bird), which did not eat; as it is written (Job 29:18): ״ואומר עם קני אגוע וכחול ארבה ימים״. Rabbi Yudan said in the name of R. Shimon: this חול lives a thousand years; at the end of a thousand years its body is consumed and its wings wither, and there remains from it the size of an egg, and it grows back its limbs. End quote." "<b>And he ate.</b> Here Adam sinned, for He had already warned him: ״ומעץ הדעת טוב ורע וגו״, and He included in it two warnings — one below and one above: below, that he not eat of the fruit of the tree of knowledge, that he not clothe himself in lusts; and above, that he not speculate after its counterpart and not be tempted to say that it is the principal thing and that there is no higher cause above it, like the view of the generation of the Dispersion. This is the sense of ״ומעץ הדעת טוב ורע לא תאכל ממנו״ — therefore it added לומר ממנו ('of it'), that is: do not separate it, and do not make of the fruit a tree in its own right, nor of the branch a root in its own right. And likewise the expression ויאכל, that is to say, that he transgressed against them both; and this is the sense of אכילה, which is the consuming and corrupting of a thing. And Scripture teaches you that he corrupted from two sides: he ate of the fruit below, and he cut off its counterpart above. And because his counterpart receives from the attribute of Judgment above, which is the left side, therefore when Adam returned from his sin he needed to bring an ox, to return to his heart that all is unified and joined above; and therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, prepared for him an ox with a single horn on its forehead, to indicate to him the oneness, so as to make known his transgression, that he sinned by cutting off and separating what is unified. And it says 'on its forehead' because it is the middle of the body, alluding to the middle line that unifies above and below; and 'horn' (קרן) connotes power (כוח), for the power of the ox is in its horns. And of this they said: the ox that the first Adam brought had one horn on its forehead, as it is written (Psalms 69:32): ״ותיטב לה׳ משור פר מקרין מפריס״. מקרין — this can be read two ways; it is written מקרן (singular). And this was the sin of the children in the wilderness, that they made the calf, and they needed to bring an ox for atonement, as it is written (Leviticus 9:3): ״קחו שעיר עזים לחטאת ועגל וכבש וגו׳״, and it is written (ibid.): ״ושור ואיל לשלמים״. (And from then onward we were commanded in the Torah to bring in Tishrei, in which Adam was created, an etrog with a lulav and its species, in order to join and unify all.) And so the Torah commanded for most sinners to bring their offering as an ox, and in all the festivals you will find that they would offer oxen, and likewise on the new moons, and the communal offering too was an ox — and all this to indicate that it is fitting for us to return with the very thing in which Adam sinned. And therefore Scripture is emphatic about the ox offering (Leviticus 4:4): ״והביא את הפר אל פתח אהל מועד״, ״ושחט את הפר לפני ה׳״. And our Sages of blessed memory expounded: Why is ״אהל מועד״ said regarding the ox more than all the other offerings? To what may the matter be compared? To the king's beloved who brought a fine gift to the king. The king said: Hang it at the entrance of my palace, so that all may know what a fine gift my beloved brought me. And the gift is repentance with love for the thing in which he sinned."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {5 "The letters ה\"ד introduce the prooftext citation; the citation given as איוב כ״ט:י״ח is Job 29:18."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:10:16.501222629Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}